 17592186128866 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129288 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050998 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129509 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129515 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281358 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281359 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281360 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281366 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383553 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389187 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389194 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389201 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389208 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389215 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128867 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130070 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130071 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130208 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130231 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130232 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130233 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130236 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130237 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1045 "The Jacobian" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130070 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048225 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129350 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "24e4f3d3-57f6-51a9-afd6-0c3e9a2f8f97" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389035 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160601" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129515 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4471fd6f-9a7d-5e2e-b974-bcbd5ed1ceb0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129515 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & >0 \\\\ -4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130070 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130231 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130070 #edb/ref 1061 25202 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5564d0a2-de60-5f46-b603-c6061aab85f2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130398 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130071 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130208 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130071 #edb/ref 1061 25204 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "838542bc-20ad-57ca-b114-1923593d1021" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129193 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130232 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1061 42421 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0954c2f4-d0e0-56be-8d88-2f803b4f5152" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130208 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129269 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130071 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130231 #edb/ref 1061 44550 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eadcf9f2-f662-5ba3-8bbc-b9166dfa1142" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130466 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130233 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1061 44557 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c956f456-bd29-5cf7-83bd-de9b757b718f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130467 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130236 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1061 44558 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e6a94d3-c32f-5582-98ea-69a8605ef653" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130468 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130237 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130236 #edb/ref 1061 44604 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b57bfb4-8aa6-5afc-b54b-581e471d245a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129288 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1061 44605 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dc69ec2-a31b-570c-8cf5-3d69b616fa0a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129289 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130398 #edb/ref 1064 "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nRecall that, under this notation,\n\n- $\\mathbf u = [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n- $\\mathbf x = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that\n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130398 #edb/ref 1065 4147 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130398 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c831da3f-8fff-5c5b-8be5-f3602a7303ba" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130398 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$\n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}v & -u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ & =u{cos}^{2}v+u{sin}^{2}v \\\\ & =u({cos}^{2}v+{sin}^{2}v) \\\\ & =u.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1065 7668 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0ea7366-d3d6-5645-99d8-72f7febb8d19" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1067 "The Jacobian Determinant" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130467 #edb/ref 1064 "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation\n\n$$\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130467 #edb/ref 1065 7672 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4dcb1ce1-bfad-524c-a89a-a589532a1067" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130467 #edb/ref 1067 "A Review of Affine Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130468 #edb/ref 1064 "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones.\n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$J(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson).\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$.\n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)< 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130468 #edb/ref 1065 7673 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3f6a2c63-d9fc-533c-8c4b-fd822216f0f1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130468 #edb/ref 1067 "Intuition Behind the Jacobian" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186275839 #edb/ref 1081 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186275839 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186275839 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "27184dd5-9306-5151-ba71-71c0184555b9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186276805 #edb/ref 1081 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186276805 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186276805 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "921684d8-1a85-53da-b8a7-23bd356d4d8f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186276986 #edb/ref 1081 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186276986 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186276986 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "a6b6ecd9-7efa-5fbf-99f4-87cf3cf2fc2b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277013 #edb/ref 1081 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277013 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277013 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "a9f4d180-9148-56f8-af89-c87393729376" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277159 #edb/ref 1081 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277159 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277159 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "bc4ef6cf-c02f-531a-934f-84c5afac9290" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275839 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276805 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276986 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277013 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277159 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186276805 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186277827 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "3f823688-c59b-5a64-9e51-9b7c368087e1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278294 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277827 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278294 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160383" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278294 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278294 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b1f8f7b-fd82-5c84-a276-7fa2fd9b071a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278294 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot (-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278603 #edb/ref 1081 "12\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278603 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278603 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "018385f7-7519-5922-8545-718d1e4443e3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278694 #edb/ref 1081 "6\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278694 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278694 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "09f72da4-93f7-5f6e-8f5c-7adf97a2d87b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279188 #edb/ref 1081 "16\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279188 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279188 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "42229dfb-92db-527f-98d4-81e464debdce" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279407 #edb/ref 1081 "9\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279407 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279407 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "5806f5cb-b442-516d-a7af-f295ed7a67a3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280197 #edb/ref 1081 "3\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280197 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280197 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "b63f49ff-6805-5e3d-9a84-bb5bf0cb46d3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278603 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278694 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279188 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279407 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280197 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186278694 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186280929 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "30341de0-d7c9-531a-935d-0764a34a14a5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186281374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280929 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186281374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161875" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186281374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186281374 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(0,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u-{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186281374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a571cf4-d621-5149-95d3-896e62b990d5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186281374 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369880 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160445" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369879 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91c905cb-29ef-5cbc-a791-764e51ba7eed" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369879 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369881 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369883 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369884 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369885 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186369881 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369880 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "c478e331-2d1a-585a-9599-198d7061ae49" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369881 #edb/ref 1081 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369881 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186369881 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "4dc0ac04-220f-5368-899c-f4b853d6caf1" #edb/ref 17592186045585]